    shortAnswer: 'HTTP forgets you between requests. A token is the wristband proving you already logged in.',
    shortAnswerHi: 'HTTP har request ke beech aapko bhool jata hai. Token wahi wristband hai jo sabit karta hai ki aap login kar chuke ho.',
    detailedAnswer: 'Each HTTP request is independent — the server has no memory of the last one. Without something extra, you would have to send your password with every single click. Instead, you log in once and the server hands back a token: a signed string meaning "this person already proved who they are". Your app sends it with each request, and the server verifies the signature. It is a wristband at an event — checked once at the entrance, shown at every stall afterwards. Because a token grants access, it must be treated like a password: sent over HTTPS, stored where scripts cannot read it, and given a short expiry.',
    detailedAnswerHi: 'Har HTTP request alag hoti hai — server ko pichhli yaad nahi rehti. Kuch extra na ho to har click ke saath password bhejna padta. Iski jagah aap ek baar login karte ho aur server ek token deta hai: signed string jiska matlab hai "is bande ne pehle hi sabit kar diya ki wo kaun hai". Aapki app har request ke saath use bhejti hai, aur server signature verify karta hai. Ye event ka wristband hai — gate par ek baar check hua, phir har stall par bas dikhana hai. Token access deta hai, isliye use password jaisa hi samjho: HTTPS par bhejo, aisi jagah rakho jahan scripts na padh sakein, aur expiry chhoti rakho.',
